  "step_3": "The decision reason focuses on the least probable outcome of each lottery and prefers the one with the higher payoff (6000 vs 3000). The individual also considers the low probability of winning and justifies choosing the option with a higher potential gain despite halved chances (0.2% vs 0.1% — A has twice the chance of B, but B offers twice the reward). The individual's reasoning aligns with focusing on the rare, high-payoff outcome and favoring it due to its higher value, even though its probability is lower. While the decision reason specifically selects based on the most favorable minimum-probability outcome (which is B), the individual appears to prefer a higher win with lower chances, which is consistent with the logic of the reason. Although the verbal report does not explicitly name the least probable outcome as a decision rule, the justification ('higher win' despite 'halved chances') reflects a focus on the high-end outcome and its desirability, which matches the core logic of the decision reason. The alignment in both considered information (high payoff, low probability) and justification (preferring higher gain despite lower odds) supports that the reason was used.",
  "final_assessment_score": 90
